Invariants (this is the real exercise):
Conservation. Total money is conserved across every transfer — no partial/torn transfers,
no lost or created paise, even under a concurrent burst.

Idempotency. Every transfer carries an idempotency_
key ; a retry with the same key re‐
turns the original outcome and does not move funds twice. Same key + a different body →
409.
Get-or-create is race-free. Two concurrent first-transfers between the same brand-new
pair must create each wallet exactly once — never two wallet rows, never a 500. (This is
the crux.)
Authorization. A caller can only spend from their own wallet and only read transfers they’re
part of — identity from the token, never a client-supplied id.
Money as integer paise (never float).
Persistence: a real database (Postgres recommended; not an in-memory map), schema via
migrations.
The thing we will probe live (your correctness gate)
Create two brand-new users. Fire many concurrent first-transfers between them at once, and
many concurrent retries of the same transfer with the same idempotency_
key. Exactly the right
amount moves, each wallet is created exactly once, retries apply once, balances reconcile —
never overspent, never a 500. Give us a one-command way (a script or curl one-liner) to
fire that burst at your live service.
Two design calls we specifically want you to reason about (in the
write-up)
1. The get-or-create + transfer race. There’s a simple, correct way to make “create the coun‐
terparty wallet if absent, then move funds” safe under concurrency, and heavier ways. Pick
the simplest correct mechanism and defend it — how do you avoid the two-concurrent-first-
requests-both-insert race (the classic find-or-create failure that 500s), and where exactly
does the idempotency key sit relative to the balance mutation?
2. Consistency vs. availability. When the database is slow or unavailable, what does the
transfer path do — reject (favor consistency: never double-spend) or degrade? And the read
path (GET balance) under load? State your NFR priorities explicitly and justify them for a
money workload — don’t just assert one.

The real focus — deploy, containerize, observe
1. Containerize properly — multi-stage Dockerfile → small non-root image, container
HEALTHCHECK; a docker-compose.yml that brings up app + db in one command; deploy
the container image (not a buildpack); 12-factor config (everything sensitive via environ‐
ment, nothing committed). Confirm it builds and its tests pass from a clean checkout before
you send it.
2. Deploy it live — a public URL on a free host (Render / Railway / Fly.io / Koyeb), backed by
free managed Postgres (Neon / Supabase / Railway). It should just work when we hit it.
3. Make it observable — a public link to view the logs. Structured JSON logs with a correla‐
tion id per request, logging the meaningful events (transfer applied, insufficient-funds reject‐
ed, idempotent replay, the concurrent request that lost the get-or-create race, auth failures).
Basic metrics (request count, latency incl. p99, error rate, transfers applied/rejected) via
/metrics and/or a dashboard.
What to send back
The live app URL, the public GitHub repo, and the public logs link.
The burst script (concurrent transfers + retries) so we can re-run your gate.
A one-page write-up we’ll read closely:
your data model;
the mechanism that makes get-or-create + transfer safe under concurrency and why it’s
the simplest correct one (name the heavier alternatives you rejected);
how idempotency keys are stored / matched / expired and same-key-different-body
detected;
how identity/authorization works (token → caller, no trusted header);
your consistency-vs-availability decision;
the edge cases you handled (insufficient funds, self-transfer, unknown recipient, replay
vs. conflict, negative/zero amount);
your containerization / deploy / observability choices;
your AI usage (directed vs. decided);
and a short cost note showing it’s all free-tier.

What we’re evaluating (and not)
Money conservation + correct idempotency under concurrency (we’ll reproduce it against your
live URL); a race-free get-or-create; token-based authorization (no trusted header); the quality
of your NFR reasoning; and that it’s genuinely deployed, containerized, and observable.
We are not grading UI, feature breadth, or exotic auth — keep the surface tiny and the reason‐
ing sharp. A small service that never loses money and reasons honestly about its trade-
offs beats a large, over-engineered one.
Looking forward to seeing it live.